eslint v 0.18 ? (demo)

``` js
/* eslint semi: 0, indent: [2,2] */
var test = "lel"
var ok
var notOk
switch(test) {
case "test":
  break;

case "assert":
  if(test) {
    ok++
  }
  else {
    notOk++
  }
}
```

```
13:0 - Expected indentation of 0 characters.
14:2 - Expected indentation of 2 characters.
15:0 - Expected indentation of 0 characters.
```

Expected:

No errors.

---

I also tested this snippet with indented cases which give same sort of errors

``` js
/* eslint semi: 0, indent: [2,2] */
var test = "lel"
var ok
var notOk
switch(test) {
  case "test":
    break;

  case "assert":
    if(test) {
      ok++
    }
    else {
      notOk++
    }
    break
}
```

```
13:2 - Expected indentation of 2 characters.
14:4 - Expected indentation of 4 characters.
15:2 - Expected indentation of 2 characters.
```

Expected: 

No errors.

My code:

```
function fun() {
    switch (1) {
        case 1:
            return 1
        default:
            return 2
    }
}
```

I want to ensure that I remembered to indent all the switch cases.
So I set up my package.json as:

```
{
    "eslintConfig": {
        "rules": {
            "indent": [ 1, "tab", { "indentSwitchCase": true } ],
            "no-unused-vars": 0,
            "semi": 0,
            "strict": 0
        }
    }
}
```

And now I get errors:

```
index.js
  5:3  warning  Expected indentation of 3 characters  indent
  6:4  warning  Expected indentation of 4 characters  indent
  7:3  warning  Expected indentation of 3 characters  indent

✖ 4 problems (1 error, 3 warnings)
```

It shouldn't expect an indentation of 3 characters. `switch` is at indentation 1, so `default` should be at indentation 2, not 3. To satisfy the rule I have to make my code look like this:

```
function fun() {
    switch (1) {
        case 1:
            return 1
            default:
                return 2
            }
}
```
